<ul class="breadcrumb">
    <li><a href="/">Schoolbox</a></li>
    <li><a href="/pantry">Pantry</a></li>
    <li><a href="/pantry/form">Forms</a></li>
    <li class="active"><a href="/pantry/form-progressive">Progressive Form</a></li>
</ul>
<div class="row">
    <div class="small-12 island">
        <h1>Pantry - Progressive Form</h1>
        <ul class="meta">
            <li class="icon-info">Sections in this form are dependent on the sections before them.</li>
            <li class="icon-star">For the magically-inclined, a progressive form is <u><strong>almost</strong></u> a Wizard. So it's more like a Mage.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 class="subheader">One Form, Many Fieldsets</h2>
        <form action="/pantry/formForm.php" method="post" data-mage-preventsubmit="true">
            <section>
                <fieldset data-mage="part-1" class="content" {% if hashUsed %}disabled="disabled"{% endif %}>
                    <legend>Here's Our First Fieldset</legend>
                    <input type="hidden" name="key" value="{{ key }}" />
                    <div class="small-12 column" data-mage-collapsefinal>
                        <p>
                            This first section starts us off, probably with core identity fields. Things like names.
                            Or if you're making a Mage, maybe it just contains introduction text like this.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Notice that you can see all the other section titles, but the content is collapsed.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Also note the DOM structure for the button and (empty) error message below.
                            For aesthetic consistency, it's kind of important.
                        </p>
                        <div class="row" data-mage-submitrow {% if hashUsed %}style="display:none;"{% endif %}>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-8 column">
                                <p class="alert alert-box icon-alert" data-mage-error></p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-4 column">
                                <input type="submit" name="part-1" value="I understand" data-mage-next="part-2" data-mage-working="Wait 2 secs..." />
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </fieldset>
                <fieldset data-mage="part-2" class="content" disabled="disabled">
                    <legend>Section Two!</legend>
                    <input type="hidden" name="key" value="{{ key }}" />
                    <div class="small-12 column {% if not hashUsed %}hide{% endif %}" data-mage-collapsed>
                        <div data-mage-collapsefinal>
                            <p>Hey, you submitted the first section! Awesome. See how the first section submit went away? Neat, right?</p>
                            <p>Here's some things you probably should know:</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    All <code>fieldset[data-mage] input[type=submit]</code> buttons <em><strong>require</strong></em>:
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>a unique <code>[name]</code> attribute and</li>
                                        <li>the <code>[data-mage-next]</code> attribute</li>
                                    </ul>
                                    to work progressively. There's no naming convention, they just need to be unique to the page.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    The <code>[data-mage-working]</code> attribute is what changed the text on the submit button while the AJAX request was taking place.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    The previous <code>fieldset</code> has now been <code>[disabled]</code>.
                                    This is the default behaviour, but that's a config setting you can change.
                                    There will also eventually be an IE polyfill to disable all the individual form elements too, because IE doesn't support fieldset disablement inheritance. Because IE.
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <label>Ooh, a text input!
                            <input type="text" name="part-2-text" value="{{ part2 }}" placeholder="Leave this blank" />
                        </label>
                        <div class="row" data-mage-submitrow {% if hashUsed %}style="display:none;"{% endif %}>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-8 column">
                                <p class="alert alert-box icon-alert" data-mage-error></p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-4 column">
                                <input type="submit" name="part-2" value="Neeeeext!" data-mage-next="part-3" data-mage-working="Asyncing your data...." />
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </fieldset>
                <fieldset data-mage="part-3" class="content" disabled="disabled">
                    <legend>Call and Response</legend>
                    <input type="hidden" name="key" value="{{ key }}" />
                    <div class="small-12 column {% if not hashUsed %}hide{% endif %}" data-mage-collapsed>

                        <p>Is this sort of like what you entered above?</p>
                        <h3 class="alert-box" data-previousinput>{{ part2result }}</h3>
                        <p data-mage-collapsefinal>
                            The AJAX result of the previous section can be used to manipulate parts of this one (or anything else, for that matter).
                            You could theoretically load entirely different sub-pages, based on input from above.
                            The callback you use for this (onXhrDone) fires before the slideDown(),
                            which lets you pipe or manipulate any part of the next section's DOM before it gets seen.
                        </p>
                        <div class="row" data-mage-submitrow {% if hashUsed %}style="display:none;"{% endif %}>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-8 column">
                                <p class="alert alert-box icon-alert" data-mage-error></p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-4 column">
                                <input type="submit" name="part-3" value="That's pretty neato" data-mage-next="part-4" data-mage-working="Working..." />
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </fieldset>
                <fieldset data-mage="part-4" class="content" {% if not hashUsed %}disabled="disabled"{% endif %}>
                    <legend>Presume to Resume</legend>
                    <input type="hidden" name="key" value="{{ key }}" />
                    <div class="small-12 column {% if not hashUsed %}hide{% endif %}" data-mage-collapsed data-mage-collapsefinal>
                        <blockquote>"But why all this hassle??"</blockquote>
                        <p>I hear you ask! Why bother indeed!</p>
                        <p>
                            There are times when you need to save a parent entity before allowing creation of sub-entities,
                            so that ID's exist and can be linked up. You can also <em>resume</em> partial forms,
                            since every time you press a submit button (every section), you're saving data to the DB (or in this example, your session).
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For example, your input to this form can be recovered by <a href="/pantry/form-progressive/{{ key }}">tacking '/{{ key }}' onto the URL</a>.<br/>
                            {% if hashUsed %}<strike>{% endif %}
                            Go on, give it a try. It will, via a synchronous page load, restore you to this point in the form.
                            {% if hashUsed %}
                                </strike><br/>See? Easy peasy. (You probably wouldn't display different output like this, but it was a way to force you to see the effect.)
                            {% endif %}
                        </p>
                        {% if hashUsed %}
                        <div class="row" data-mage-submitrow>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-8 column">
                                <p class="alert alert-box icon-alert" data-mage-error></p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="small-12 medium-4 column">
                                <input type="submit" name="part-4" value="Riiight" data-mage-next="part-5" data-mage-working="Working..." />
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        {% endif %}
                    </div>
                </fieldset>
                <fieldset data-mage="part-5" class="content" disabled="disabled">
                    <legend>Backward in Going Forward</legend>
                    <input type="hidden" name="key" value="{{ key }}" />
                    <div class="small-12 column hide" data-mage-collapsed>
                        <p>Did you make a mistake in one of the inputs? Need to make a change?</p>
                        <p>Right now, the only mechanism to 'revert' is via a resume-point, but future functionality could include the ability to restore a form section.</p>
                        <p>In this final step, there's a callback that collapses all non-input content, so you get the final overview of what you're about to submit and save.</p>
                    </div>
                    <!-- Notable absence: no [data-mage-submitrow] since this is the final section -->
                </fieldset>
            </section>
            <div class="row" data-mage="part-5">
                <div class="small-12 column hide" data-mage-collapsed>
                    <ul class="meta">
                        <li class="icon-info">This final submit isn't technically part of the progressive, so it doesn't strictly need a <code>[name]</code></li>
                    </ul>
                    <input type="submit" value="Finish, Save, Done." />
                </div>
            </div>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>
<script type="text/javascript" src="/javascript/shared/jquery.collapsable.js"></script>
<script type="text/javascript" src="/javascript/shared/jquery.mage.js"></script>
<script type="text/javascript">
    $(function () {
        // the mage plugin is applied to the submit buttons: setup a basic mage on all the buttons
        $('input[data-mage-next]').mage();

        // overload with specific callbacks (or other config values)
        $('input[data-mage-next="part-3"]').mage({
            onXhrDone: function (data) {
                // $.fn.mage('next|target') will return you the target of this button, which is the "next" section
                $(this).mage('next').eq(0).find('[data-previousinput]').text(data);
            }
        });

        $('input[data-mage-next="part-5"]').mage({
            onXhrDone: function (data) {
                $('fieldset[data-mage]').find('[data-mage-collapsefinal]').slideUp();
                $(this).closest('form').removeAttr('data-mage-preventsubmit');
            }
        });
    });
</script>
